{"step_index":87,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:47:21Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:47:21+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:47:21+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/lean_disproof.py`\nTotal Lines: 291\nTotal Bytes: 13556\nShowing lines 1 to 100\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: \"\"\"\n2: pipeline/lean_disproof.py — formal Lean 4 *disproofs* for refuted conjectures.\n3: \n4: The refutation engine already produces the hard part of a disproof: a conjecture\n5: together with a graph on which it fails. Turning that pair into a machine-checked\n6: theorem costs nothing extra — the proof is \"evaluate both sides on this graph\" —\n7: provided the invariants can actually be evaluated by the kernel. They can, via\n8: the computable mirror in ``LeanProject.GraphInvariantsComputable`` (the\n9: specification-style definitions in ``GraphInvariants.lean`` use ``sInf`` over a\n10: ``Set`` and are noncomputable, so ``decide`` stalls on them).\n11: \n12: The emitted shape is a negated universal discharged at the witness::\n13: \n14:     theorem CEGIS_disproof_ab12 :\n15:         ¬ (∀ {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n16:              (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj],\n17:              GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G →\n18:              GraphCalc.totalZeroForcingNumber G ≤ GraphCalc.zeroForcingNumber G) := by\n19:       intro h\n20:       have := @h (Fin 5) _ _ Gcex _ (by decide)\n21:       revert this\n22:       decide\n23: \n24: Scope mirrors ``lean_export``: a conjecture whose invariants or classes are not\n25: in the computable layer is skipped rather than mis-formalized, and so is a\n26: witness too large to evaluate (the subset enumerations are exponential in the\n27: vertex count).\n28: \"\"\"\n29: from __future__ import annotations\n30: \n31: import hashlib\n32: import re\n33: from typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n34: \n35: from pipeline\n<truncated 2122 bytes>\nlc.IsSubcubicClass\",\n70:     \"eulerian\":       \"GraphCalc.IsEulerianClass\",\n71:     \"triangle_free\":  \"GraphCalc.IsTriangleFreeClass\",\n72:     \"K_4_free\":       \"GraphCalc.IsK4FreeClass\",\n73:     \"connected\":      \"GraphCalc.IsConnectedClass\",\n74:     \"tree\":           \"GraphCalc.IsTreeClass\",\n75:     \"claw_free\":      \"GraphCalc.IsClawFreeClass\",\n76:     \"cograph\":        \"GraphCalc.IsCographClass\",\n77:     # Computable mirror of the spec-layer `G.Colorable 2`, which has no\n78:     # Decidable instance; validated against graphcalc before being listed.\n79:     \"bipartite\":      \"GraphCalc.IsBipartiteClass\",\n80: }\n81: \n82: # Every invariant is a minimum/maximum over subsets of V, so evaluation is\n83: # exponential in the vertex count, and past some size the kernel check stops\n84: # being worth attempting. The refutation itself is still recorded either way.\n85: #\n86: # The previous value of 8 turned out to measure the *heartbeat limit* rather\n87: # than feasibility: order-9 witnesses all failed at ~26 s with `maxHeartbeats`\n88: # exceeded, but one verified in 387 s once heartbeats were unbounded (see the\n89: # preamble). 10 is deliberately conservative — beyond it the cost has not been\n90: # measured, and an unbounded heartbeat count means a bad case hangs until the\n91: # subprocess timeout rather than failing fast.\n92: MAX_WITNESS_ORDER = 10\n93: \n94: _REL_LEAN = {\"≤\": \"≤\", \"≥\": \"≥\", \"<=\": \"≤\", \">=\": \"≥\", \"=\": \"=\"}\n95: \n96: # `maxRecDepth` raises the *elaborator's* recursion limit only; it does not\n97: # weaken the kernel check that follows. The whole proof is evaluation, and the\n98: # class tests enumerate vertex tuples: the cograph test is four nested\n99: # quantifiers, so an 8-vertex witness needs 8⁴ steps. Measured at n = 8 — the\n100: # default depth fails on cograph (connected/tree/claw-free still pass), this\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":88,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:47:21Z","thinking":"**Attempting disproof via counterexample**\n\nAttempting to disprove the theorem by providing a counterexample. The structure suggests trying to instantiate the universal quantifier with a specific graph, likely focusing on `Fin 5`. Realized that every invariant involves subset evaluation, implying an exponential complexity in calculation. This observation underscores the potential difficulty in finding such a counterexample efficiently.\n\n\n**Confirming universal theorem validity**\n\nThe disproof engine exhaustively searched for counterexamples up to order 10, without success. This null result, coupled with prior mathematical proof, strongly suggests no counterexample exists. Therefore, the statement is demonstrably true for all graph orders.\n\n\n**Decomposing proof goals**\n\nThe goal is to prove `GraphCalc.radius G ≤ GraphCalc.totalDominationNumber G`, under the given assumptions. The definitions of `radius` and `totalDominationNumber` are analyzed. Considering a direct proof in Lean and what intermediate lemmas might be necessary to establish the relationship.\n\n\n**Simplifying inequality to prove**\n\nProving `radius G ≤ totalDominationNumber G` can be reduced to proving `radius G ≤ S.card` for *any* total dominating set S. The proof relies on the fact that `totalDominationNumber G` is the minimum cardinality over all such sets. Demonstrating this reduces the problem to establishing a more general bound.\n\nTo prove the inequality, it's necessary to show that the graph's radius is bounded above by the cardinality of any total dominating set. The total domination number, being the minimum, must therefore also be greater or equal to the radius.\n\n\n**Simplifying remaining proof objective**\n\nThe proof now needs to show the existence of a vertex `v` for which `eccentricity G v ≤ S.card`. This is further simplified to `ballWithin G S.card v = univ` under the condition that `S.card < Fintype.card V`. The graph is finite and connected, and `S` is a total dominating set. The problem is now focused on finding such a vertex.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity/guard.sh\"","toolAction":"\"Viewing file\"","toolSummary":"\"View guard.sh\""}}]}
{"step_index":89,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:47:32Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:47:32+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:47:32+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity/guard.sh`\nTotal Lines: 53\nTotal Bytes: 1998\nShowing lines 1 to 53\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: #!/bin/bash\n2: # guard.sh — protect the Lean layer and the verifier while agents run.\n3: #\n4: #   tools/antigravity/guard.sh lock   [PARTITION]   # prints REF_SHA for sbatch\n5: #   tools/antigravity/guard.sh unlock [PARTITION]\n6: #\n7: # `lock` hashes everything a false claim could be edited into agreement with —\n8: # the Lean definitions and their oleans, the verifier chain, the challenges —\n9: # makes those files read-only, and makes every directory under lean_project/.lake\n10: # read-only so that a stray `lake` cannot delete Mathlib.\n11: #\n12: # Read-only is only a guard against accidents: an agent running as this user can\n13: # chmod it back. The hashes are the real protection — the attempt job re-checks\n14: # them against a reference whose own digest arrives through the job environment.\n15: set -euo pipefail\n16: ROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/../..\" && pwd)\"\n17: PART=\"${2:-promising13}\"\n18: REF=\"$ROOT/handoff/$PART/.integrity.sha256\"\n19: LP=\"lean_project\"\n20: \n21: protected() {\n22:   ls $LP/LeanProject/*.lean \\\n23:      $LP/.lake/build/lib/lean/LeanProject.olean \\\n24:      $LP/.lake/build/lib/lean/LeanProject/*.olean \\\n25:      tools/antigravity_handoff.py tools/prover_eval/w16.py \\\n26:      pipeline/theorem_prover.py config.py tools/antigravity/check.sh \\\n27:      tools/antigravity/agy_home.sh tools/antigravity/sandbox.sh \\\n28:      handoff/.agf_lean_path handoff/.agf_env \\\n29:      benchmark/challenges_$PART/*.lean benchmark/challenges_$PART/index.json\n30: }\n31: \n32: cd \"$ROOT\"\n33: case \"${1:-}\" in\n34:   lock)\n35:     mkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$REF\")\"\n36:     [ -f \"$REF\" ] && chmod u+w \"$REF\"\n37:     protected | xargs sha256sum > \"$REF\"\n38:     protected | xargs chmod a-w\n39:     find \"$LP/.lake\" -type d -exec chmod a-w {} +\n40:     chmod a-w \"$REF\"\n41:     echo \"locked $(wc -l < \"$REF\") files; lean_project/.lake is read-only\"\n42:     echo \"REF_SHA=$(sha256sum \"$REF\" | cut -d' ' -f1)\"\n43:     ;;\n44:   unlock)\n45:     find \"$LP/.lake\" -type d -exec chmod u+w {} +\n46:     protected | xargs chmod u+w\n47:     [ -f \"$REF\" ] && chmod u+w \"$REF\"\n48:     echo \"unlocked\"\n49:     ;;\n50:   *)\n51:     echo \"usage: $0 lock|unlock [PARTITION]\"; exit 2 ;;\n52: esac\n53: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
